## Behavioral Contract

- The base image is clean and exhibits the intended correct behavior.
- The output image introduces exactly the fault supported by the description.
- `repro.sh` exits 0 only when the expected pre-fix symptom is observed.
- `repro.sh` must execute the real failing software command and assert its exit
  code and output. Do not merely inspect configuration text and print a
  synthetic copy of the expected error.
- `f2p.sh` fails before the repair and passes after it.
- `p2p.sh` passes both before and after repair and blocks obvious shortcuts.
- The host runs F2P and P2P against the faulty image before applying the gold
  patch. Every F2P script must fail there and every P2P script must pass.
- P2P may assert only behavior or configuration that genuinely exists before
  the repair and must remain afterward. Do not require an undeclared option,
  the repaired value, or an incidental implementation detail.
- `gold_patch.diff` changes only allowed configuration files.
- `eval_patch.sh` dynamically extracts those files from a fresh faulty
  container, checks patch scope, applies the patch, starts another fresh
  container, waits for readiness, and runs F2P plus P2P.
- `eval_patch.sh` must accept exactly the host runner interface
  `eval_patch.sh <prediction-patch> [image-name]`. Read `$1` as the patch path
  and `${2:-{{ instance_id | lower }}:latest}` as the image. Do not rely on the
  current working directory or a hard-coded `meta/gold_patch.diff`.
- The patch argument may be absolute. Normalize it before changing directories:
  `patch_file="$(cd "$(dirname "$1")" && pwd)/$(basename "$1")"`. Never prefix
  an absolute patch path with `$PWD`.
- Host evaluator scripts must run on macOS and Linux. Do not use GNU-only
  extensions such as `find -printf`, `readlink -f`, or `sed -r`. Prefer Git
  commands, POSIX utilities, and shell parameter expansion.
- Harnesses, gold patches, and private metadata must never be copied into the
  image.

Use `#!/usr/bin/env bash` for every script that uses `set -euo pipefail`; do not
combine `#!/bin/sh` with `pipefail`. Avoid sleeps as the primary readiness
mechanism. Pin software versions where practical. Keep credentials synthetic
and isolated. Check that every non-POSIX diagnostic command used by a harness
exists in the selected image; a missing command must fail the harness rather
than accidentally satisfy a negated condition.

The final container must remain running and provide an investigation shell even
when the target service itself fails to start. `runtime.ready_check` describes
when the container is ready for the fixer agent; it must not wait for a service
whose absence is the intended symptom.

Before finishing, read both generated JSON files back and compare every field
name and nesting level with the exact contracts above. Finish only after all
required files are present and internally consistent.
